WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:26.380
Everyone and their dog know about Russian
scientist Ivan Pavlov and, well, his dog,

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:30.100
and how he trained him to drool on command
at the sounding of a bell.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:35.050
His discovery of conditioned reflexes won
him a Nobel Prize in Psychology.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.660
Pretty straightforward, right?

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:37.660
Wrong!

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:43.410
Much of what we know about Pavlov’s experiment
has been misrepresented and mistranslated,

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:48.920
so join me in today’s Biographics to learn
the truth behind Ivan Pavlov’s research

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.770
and his fascinating life.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:56.930
A word of warning: if you happen to watch
these videos with your canine friends, this

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:00.379
is one where you may want to cover their ears…

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:08.649
A Boy’s Favourite Uncle
Ivan Petrovich Pavlov was born in Ryazan,

00:01:08.649 --> 00:01:14.210
in the Russian Empire, about 200 miles from
Moscow on the September 26, 1849.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:19.940
Ivan’s father, Petr, was the local Orthodox
Parish priest, a well-liked and respected

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:24.120
figure who supplemented the family’s income
by growing his own vegetables and brewing

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.670
his own vodka-based fruity liquor.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:33.119
Ivan was a frail and sickly child, one you
might immediately assign to the role of bookworm.

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:37.280
Except that he had absolutely no power of
concentration, hated reading and had a pretty

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.539
volatile temper.

00:01:38.539 --> 00:01:43.200
In fact, he avoided homework as often as he
could, preferring to help Petr in tending

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.560
to his plot of land.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.709
At the age of eight, Ivan suffered a bad fall,
plunging from a tall fence onto a stone slab.

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:54.140
As he struggled to recover from his injuries,
his godfather, also a priest, stepped in.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:59.040
For some reason, he believed that what the
boy needed was not a proper trauma assessment

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.940
or a doctor, but
“Discipline, discipline and more discipline”

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:08.110
So he took Ivan to his monastery, where for
several months the boy lived like a monk.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:12.510
The therapy did produce some results; bored
out of his mind, Ivan started doing something

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.080
he had never considered: reading!

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:20.250
With little other recourse, the young boy
learned to enjoy it, devouring the monastery’s

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.250
library.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:23.900
When he returned home, his personality had
changed.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.540
He was calmer, more focused, and had a great
desire to learn.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:33.150
Petr reignite his hopes of sending Ivan to
seminary to become a new addition to the Pavlov

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.180
Family’s roster of clergymen.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:39.810
The other pivotal moment in young Ivan’s
life was when his uncle -- also a priest,

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.709
also named Ivan -- moved in with the family.

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:48.610
If Petr wanted to provide his son with strong
role models, he had done a rubbish job, as

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.299
Uncle Ivan was a terrible priest.

00:02:51.299 --> 00:02:55.780
Shortly after he was assigned his most recent
church, local parishioners had started reporting

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.049
ghastly sightings of a white, robed figure
stalking the graveyard.

00:03:00.049 --> 00:03:03.540
Bodies were dug out from their tombs and simply
disappeared.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.969
The church bells had the habit of going off
at the most ungodly hours of the night.

00:03:07.969 --> 00:03:12.700
Of course, it was all thanks to Uncle Ivan,
who enjoyed playing a prank or two while drunk

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.230
like a baboon at a bachelor party.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:19.930
When the parishioners found out, they cornered
Uncle Ivan, thrashed him like a snare drum

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:24.510
at a Napalm Death recording session and kicked
him out of the village.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:28.939
Uncle Ivan’s only choice was to move in
with his brother Petr, where apparently he

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:32.060
sobered up and developed a strong bond with
little Ivan.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:36.200
The boy loved the elder Ivan, from whom he
heard stories about his other uncle -- also

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.629
a priest, also named Ivan.

00:03:38.629 --> 00:03:44.030
Because why choose new names when you can
confuse amateur historians instead?!

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:47.129
Now, about Uncle Ivan II.

00:03:47.129 --> 00:03:48.950
Was he a good priest?

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:49.950
Not really.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:53.730
His favourite past-time was a sort of brawling
much in fashion during those days.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:58.230
Basically, the men of one street, or an entire
village, would fill up on enough vodka to

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:05.110
floor a company of Varangians, then challenged
a rival team of equally intoxicated sluggers.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.189
Think of it as a Russian donnybrook.

00:04:07.189 --> 00:04:12.750
Now, I don’t know about you, but my local
priest only drank weak tea and a glass of

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.010
port on Christmas Day, and he only challenged
the village postman at chequers.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:19.060
Anyhow, Uncle Ivan II once took his brawl
too far, and he died as a result of his injuries.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:24.250
I am not sure if such role models would encourage
a young mind to take up priesthood, but these

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:31.200
experiences surely taught a vital lesson to
young Ivan: the importance of moderation and

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.400
the avoidance of all excesses in life.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.970
With a strong moral compass installed by the
tales of his elder Ivans, Pavlov was finally

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.600
sent to study at a seminary.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:45.140
His experience at the religious school started
promisingly, but soon Ivan was distracted

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:50.650
by the irresistible allure of a demanding
mistress: science.

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:55.710
During the early 1860s, Russian society had
initiated a period of economic, social, and

00:04:55.710 --> 00:05:02.270
cultural reforms promoted by Tsar Alexander
II, who ascended to the throne in 1855.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:07.910
Most notable was the abolition of serfdom,
which totally revolutionized the Russian economy.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:12.400
Another key element of these reforms was the
promotion of science to foster modernisation

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.400
of the State.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:17.820
As a result, local public libraries were stocked
with scientific works by both Russian and

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.410
foreign author previously unavailable.

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:25.390
Ryazan’s public library was flooded by Ivan
and his friends, who had formed a discussion

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:29.050
circle to review and analyse modern scientific
discussions.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:33.080
Works like Charles Darwin’s The Origin of
the Species or the journals by radical scientist

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:34.470
Dmitry Pisarev.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:40.220
One book would be particularly relevant in
Ivan’s future career: Reflexes of the Brain

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:45.470
by Ivan Sechenov, in which the author argued
that all human behaviours and thoughts could

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:50.060
be explained as a result of machine-like reflexes
and reactions.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:53.970
Young Ivan’s thirst for knowledge was so
avid that he would not allow any obstacle

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.050
to come in his way.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:58.940
If the seminary demanded that he studied all
day, he would read at night or very early

00:05:58.940 --> 00:05:59.940
in the morning.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:04.350
When access to the library became so crowded
that fistfights exploded regularly, he convinced

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:08.540
a librarian to keep a window always open at
the back – so that he could climb into a

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:12.790
reading room undisturbed and get on with his
extra-curricular studies.

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:17.390
One of the Karamazovs?

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:24.150
In 1869, Ivan could no longer continue his
ruse of progressing his religious and scientific

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.050
studies along parallel tracks.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:30.240
He had to let go of something, and that something
was religion.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.500
He quit the seminary and enrolled at the University
of St. Petersburg to study chemistry and physiology

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:40.310
– the branch of biology that deals with
the normal functions of living organisms and

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:41.310
their parts.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:45.530
Petr Pavlov was not pleased with this decision,
but Ivan had put his foot down.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:50.590
He would stand by his rational choices, regardless
if these would sour his personal relationships.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:55.670
In a later letter to his future wife, Ivan
compared himself to one of the Brothers Karamazov,

00:06:55.670 --> 00:07:01.280
from the novel by Fedor Dostoyevsky: one of
the brothers, also named Ivan, was a rationalist

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:06.170
and an extreme skeptic, condemned to social
isolation for his nihilism.

00:07:06.170 --> 00:07:11.750
By 1875, Ivan had earned a degree and a doctorate
from the Imperial Medical Academy, and continued

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:16.200
his studies under renowned physiologists Rudolf
Heidenhain and Carl Ludwig.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:21.800
At the same time, he had enrolled at the Academy
of Medical Surgery, where in 1879 he attained

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:25.820
his third degree and was awarded a gold medal
for academic results.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:31.170
Ivan’s studies were crowned by his doctor’s
thesis, published in 1883.

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:34.310
Its title was
“The Centrifugal Nerves of the Heart”

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:40.550
Which sounds like a bad Bonnie Tyler outtake,
but was actually a pioneering work in which

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:44.640
he laid down the basic principles of the trophic
function of the nervous system.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.930
In other words: the role of the nervous system
in nutrition.

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:52.750
In the meanwhile, Ivan had married a teacher
called Seraphima Karchevskaya, also known

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:54.590
as Sara, in 1881.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:57.140
Their first years together were not happy.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:08:01.390
Initially, the couple lived in almost complete
poverty, while Ivan was still perfecting his

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:02.390
studies.

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:06.320
They had to frequently crash at friends’s
houses or rent bug-infested attics.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:10.600
Tragically, Sara’s first pregnancy ended
in a miscarriage; according to the doctors

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:15.840
of the time, this was caused by Sara’s constant
attempts to keep up with Ivan, a very fast

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:16.840
walker.

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:17.840
It seems more likely that the couple’s unhealthy
living conditions played a role.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:21.440
Ivan and Sara soon tried to have another child,
and Wirchik was born shortly afterward.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:24.840
Unfortunately, the baby boy died suddenly
while still an infant.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.720
Despite their grief, the couple’s life improved
in the following years.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:32.760
In 1890, Ivan received a series of appointments
which improved their financial situation.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:37.589
He was first invited to direct the Department
of Physiology at the Institute of Experimental

00:08:37.589 --> 00:08:42.110
Medicine, a post he would hold for the next
45 years; then, he was hired by the Military

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:47.810
Medical Academy, first as Professor of Pharmacology,
then as Chair of Physiology.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:50.589
All that hard work and study had eventually
paid off.

00:08:50.589 --> 00:08:53.550
Ivan and Sara were also blessed with four
healthy children, Vladimir, Victor, Vsevolod

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.070
and Vera.

00:08:56.070 --> 00:09:00.720
With his professional and personal life on
track, Ivan Pavlov launched into the research

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.529
that really mattered to him, the one on the
physiology of digestion.

00:09:05.529 --> 00:09:11.339
This took place at the Institute of Experimental
Medicine, from 1891 to 1900, and would make

00:09:11.339 --> 00:09:12.350
him a household name.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:13.350
[Note to Simon: this could be a natural point
to insert the sponsor message.

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:14.350
E.g.

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:16.699
“But before we look at Pavlov’s brilliant
hands on approach to science, how about you

00:09:16.699 --> 00:09:20.610
take a hands-on approach to knowledge with
Brilliant?”

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:24.800
– assuming that’s the sponsor.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:34.020
Or
“Unlike Ivan, you don’t need nine years

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:41.680
to find out about how the digestive system
works, in fact 15 minutes would be enough.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:44.529
Try Blinkist etc etc” if that’s the sponsor.

00:09:44.529 --> 00:09:47.199
Or:
“Before we get into Ivan’s experiments

00:09:47.199 --> 00:09:52.110
on dogs, let me tell you what I feel like
doing to people who harm dogs.

00:09:52.110 --> 00:10:05.769
That’s right: shoot them with 
a tank!

00:10:05.769 --> 00:10:11.120
And you know where you can practice with a
tank?

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:13.200
That’s right, War Thunder!”.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:27.040
If that’s the unlikely sponsor]
A man’s best friend,

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:37.050
a dog’s worst enemy
OK, back to Ivan Pavlov and his experiments.

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:38.990
First, let’s cover his methodology.

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:44.939
It may sound commonplace today that science
should be grounded on data produced via experimentation,

00:10:44.939 --> 00:10:50.040
but when Pavlov emerged as a notable scientist,
this wasn’t necessarily a given when it

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:51.670
came to physiology.

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:57.100
Pavlov was the first physiologist in the Russian
Empire to devote himself to repeated experimentation,

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:59.829
applying a factory-like approach to his lab.

00:10:59.829 --> 00:11:03.361
Pavlov recognised that meaningful changes
in the normal functions of a living organism

00:11:03.361 --> 00:11:05.959
could be assessed only over time.

00:11:05.959 --> 00:11:10.459
Pavlov’s contemporaries would experiment
on an animal, then discard it, or even kill

00:11:10.459 --> 00:11:12.640
it once the experiment was done.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:16.800
Pavlov instead preferred to keep his animals
alive -- especially dogs -- in order to document

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:19.819
physiological changes over a longer time span.

00:11:19.819 --> 00:11:23.129
He referred to this method as “chronic experiments”.

00:11:23.129 --> 00:11:29.509
And this is the moment when you may want to
cover your dog’s ears, or send them playing

00:11:29.509 --> 00:11:30.509
somewhere else.

00:11:30.509 --> 00:11:34.269
If Pavlov’s lab was a factory, then the
dogs were his machines.

00:11:34.269 --> 00:11:39.290
He performed surgical operations on every
test subject, opening a ‘fistula’ along

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:40.860
their digestive traits.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:45.960
A fistula is an opening in a living organism
which allows one to observe the inner workings

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:47.410
of its organs.

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:52.420
Pavlov needed these fistulas opened for two
reasons: 1) to observe the functioning of

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:57.870
his dogs’ digestive functions, in particular
the production of gastric juices; and 2) to

00:11:57.870 --> 00:11:59.160
collect said secretions.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:04.970
So, yeah, Ivan Pavlov may have not systematically
killed his lab animals, but their treatment

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:10.259
wasn’t a walk in the park – which I suppose
is what they were probably hoping for, considering

00:12:10.259 --> 00:12:11.259
they were dogs.

00:12:11.259 --> 00:12:15.899
It was a treatment that was painful, at best,
and caused the death of the subjects at worst.

00:12:15.899 --> 00:12:19.670
Particularly in his early experiments, Pavlov
was constantly stymied by the difficulty of

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:22.610
keeping his subjects alive after operating
on them.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:27.290
Even if one of his ‘machines’ tragically
died, Ivan was always ready to replace them.

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:31.720
But what exactly did this factory, or lab,
produced?

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:35.259
Pavlov and his team cranked out scientific
papers on the physiology of the digestive

00:12:35.259 --> 00:12:41.610
system, new research techniques, and an important
by-product: gastric juice – litres of it.

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:46.319
Ivan Pavlov was always strapped for research
cash and he had stumbled upon a lucrative

00:12:46.319 --> 00:12:50.580
side gig: Russian patients suffering from
dyspepsia were ready to pay good money for

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:55.019
canine gastric juice, considered an effective
treatment at that time.

00:12:55.019 --> 00:12:58.680
Dyspepsia is more commonly referred to as
‘indigestion’ and includes symptoms such

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:00.100
as bloating, discomfort, nausea.

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:06.660
So yeah, if you are feeling nauseous and bloated,
what better remedy than gulping down a nice

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:12.970
glass of warm, thick, moist, pungent gastric
juice freshly harvested from a fistulated

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:13.970
drooling dog?!?

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:17.470
Can you give me a minute, I think I am going
to be si-

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:19.740
[Editing note: screen cuts abruptly to black,
with a short burst of static noise.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:20.740
The camera then returns to Simon, who continues
as if nothing had happened]

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:25.670
Pavlov had the perfect set up for his indigestion
medicine factory: five large young dogs, selected

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:29.100
for their voracious appetites, were harnessed
on a long table.

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:34.490
Each dog was subjected to an incision of the
oesophagus: this opened a fistula, from which

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:36.910
a tube led to the collection vessel.

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:42.199
Each of these dogs faced a stand, tilted to
display a large bowl of minced meat, just

00:13:42.199 --> 00:13:43.199
out of reach.

00:13:43.199 --> 00:13:46.730
This stimulated the production of saliva and
gastric juices.

00:13:46.730 --> 00:13:51.110
By 1904, Pavlov’s side gig was selling more
than three thousand flagons of dog gastric

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:58.980
juice per year, raking in profits that would
increase his lab budget by 70 percent.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:04.879
The Experiment
Besides capitalising on his own brand of furry

00:14:04.879 --> 00:14:10.100
Taylorism, Pavlov began his research of what
he called “psychic secretions” – which

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:15.249
sounds like something Dr Stantz or Dr Spengler
may want to analyse, too.

00:14:15.249 --> 00:14:20.269
But in simple terms, psychic secretions were
drool produced by anything other than direct

00:14:20.269 --> 00:14:21.750
exposure to food.

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:24.589
And here we get to Pavlov’s famous experiments.

00:14:24.589 --> 00:14:29.750
Just to clarify – it wasn’t a single occurrence
in which he rang a bell to get a dog to salivate,

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:35.179
as sometimes thought by modern popular culture,
but a ‘chronic experiment’ in line with

00:14:35.179 --> 00:14:36.980
his scientific doctrine.

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:38.470
This is how it worked.

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:42.990
Pavlov’s main research was meant to study
the salivation of dogs in response to being

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:43.990
fed.

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:47.240
However, he soon noticed that the dogs would
begin salivating when they heard the footsteps

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:49.569
of the assistant bringing their food.

00:14:49.569 --> 00:14:53.959
Pavlov’s hypothesis was that any object
or event which the dogs learned to associate

00:14:53.959 --> 00:14:57.490
with food would trigger the same response
as the food itself.

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:02.089
He started from the idea that dogs – or
other complex organisms – were hard-wired

00:15:02.089 --> 00:15:04.779
to respond instinctively to certain stimuli.

00:15:04.779 --> 00:15:10.540
In other words: an ‘unconditioned stimulus’,
for example: food, will elicit an ‘unconditioned

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:12.339
response,’ or salivation.

00:15:12.339 --> 00:15:14.499
Dogs will salivate in the presence of food.

00:15:14.499 --> 00:15:16.850
It’s not a behaviour they need to learn.

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:19.200
[Editing suggestion: introduce some graphics
to illustrate the various steps described

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:20.200
here:]
[Unconditioned Stimulus] -&gt; [Unconditioned

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.110
response]
Pavlov then devised a series of experiments

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:24.129
to validate his initial hypothesis.

00:15:24.129 --> 00:15:28.890
The first step was to introduce a neutral
stimulus: this could be a metronome or an

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:34.170
electric buzzer – which in a mistranslated
paper became the famous bell.

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:37.399
The neutral stimulus did not elicit any response.

00:15:37.399 --> 00:15:42.720
[Neutral Stimulus] -&gt; [No conditioned response]
Next, it was time to begin the conditioning

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:48.350
procedure, that is associating the neutral
stimulus with the unconditioned stimulus.

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:53.339
In this example: introducing a clicking metronome
before the dog chow was served.

00:15:53.339 --> 00:15:56.920
Finally, Pavlov played the metronome, but
failed to produce the food.

00:15:56.920 --> 00:16:00.649
I am sure the dogs were dead disappointed,
but what mattered to the scientist is that

00:16:00.649 --> 00:16:03.009
the clicking in itself … did increase the
drooling!

00:16:03.009 --> 00:16:09.819
So now a conditioned stimulus had elicited
a conditioned response, also described as

00:16:09.819 --> 00:16:14.160
‘conditional’ response in some translations
from the original Russian articles.

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:21.019
[Conditioned Stimulus] -&gt; [Conditioned response]
Pavlov’s experiments would become more sophisticated:

00:16:21.019 --> 00:16:26.110
for example, he tried playing around with
specific metronome speeds, like serving food

00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:28.370
only when it clicked at sixty beats per minute.

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:32.869
Over time, his dogs became so specifically
conditioned that they failed to salivate at

00:16:32.869 --> 00:16:34.699
any other setting.

00:16:34.699 --> 00:16:38.220
Pavlov began to deduce that there were colliding
forces of “excitation” and “inhibition”

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:39.220
at play.

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:44.721
At first, a stimulus may spread broadly across
the cerebral cortex, or the area of the brain

00:16:44.721 --> 00:16:50.559
responsible for processing inputs and maintaining
cognitive functions.

00:16:50.559 --> 00:16:54.709
Later, in a potential second phase of selective
reinforcement, the stimulus could be concentrated

00:16:54.709 --> 00:16:57.370
on one specific spot of the cortex.

00:16:57.370 --> 00:17:02.160
I should reinforce here that Pavlov’s interest
was always grounded in physiology and in the

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:04.410
understanding of the digestive system.

00:17:04.410 --> 00:17:09.890
However, his work did have a huge impact on
the field of psychology: Pavlov’s discoveries

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:14.980
pushed behavioural psychologists into developing
the concept of ‘classical conditioning’

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:17.510
also known as ‘Pavlovian conditioning’.

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:22.939
This is a process of learning by association,
which, according to behaviourists, can explain

00:17:22.939 --> 00:17:25.500
all aspects of human psychology.

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:30.630
In the 1920s, psychologist John Watson went
as far as denying completely the existence

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:35.610
of the mind, or consciousness, believing that
individual behaviours, speech patterns, and

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:41.769
emotional responses are simply a consequence
of external stimuli which elicit certain responses

00:17:41.769 --> 00:17:42.779
via classical conditioning.

00:17:42.779 --> 00:17:46.810
Such an approach, taken to the extreme, negates
subjective experience.

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:50.769
In other words, ten different people, exposed
to the same input, would produce the same

00:17:50.769 --> 00:17:51.769
output.

00:17:51.769 --> 00:17:55.909
Interestingly, Pavlov never approved of the
theories that behaviourists had built on the

00:17:55.909 --> 00:17:57.150
back of his experiments.

00:17:57.150 --> 00:18:01.980
The Russian scientist firmly believed in the
importance of individuals in determining their

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:02.980
behaviour.

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:07.299
And while he, as a physiologist, did not have
the answers, he hoped science would one day

00:18:07.299 --> 00:18:11.460
explain
“the mechanism and vital meaning of that

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:14.990
which most occupied Man—our consciousness
and its torments.”

00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:24.070
Drooling over food, Spitting over the USSR
In 1903, at the 14th International Medical

00:18:24.070 --> 00:18:29.570
Congress in Madrid, Pavlov read a paper on
«The Experimental Psychology and Psychopathology

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:30.620
of Animals».

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:35.799
His research was far from completed or exhaustive,
yet his findings took the scientific community

00:18:35.799 --> 00:18:36.799
by storm.

00:18:36.799 --> 00:18:41.370
He only had to wait one year before he was
awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:44.770
1904
"in recognition of his work on the physiology

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:50.289
of digestion, through which knowledge on vital
aspects of the subject has been transformed

00:18:50.289 --> 00:18:51.299
and enlarged."

00:18:51.299 --> 00:18:54.700
From then on, the laurels piled upon Ivan’s
head.

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:59.110
In 1907, he was elected Academician of the
Russian Academy of Sciences; in 1912, he was

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:03.970
given an honorary doctorate at Cambridge University;
in 1915, he was made a member of the French

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:08.600
Order of the Legion of Honour, following a
lobbying campaign by the Medical Academy of

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:09.600
Paris.

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:13.559
Which takes us just two years before a certain
event which shook the World …

00:19:13.559 --> 00:19:19.039
When the October Revolution of 1917 ousted
Kerensky’s Government and Lenin rose to

00:19:19.039 --> 00:19:22.809
power, Pavlov was sixty-eight and had been
a Nobel Laureate for 13 years.

00:19:22.809 --> 00:19:27.240
He had had no particular sympathies for the
Tsarist regime, which had been notoriously

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:29.490
stingy when it came to funding research.

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:33.650
He wasn’t terribly fond of communism, either,
and would not hide his feelings about it.

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:39.080
Ivan Pavlov was always vocal in his opposition
to communism and its Russian incarnations.

00:19:39.080 --> 00:19:42.820
He only had two words to spare for Karl Marx:
“That fool”

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:45.740
And he wasn’t any kinder to the leaders
of the Bolshevik Revolution.

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:51.480
And yet his international standing was unassailable,
and Ivan could get away with the most outspoken

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:53.900
and ferocious critiques of the regime.

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:57.190
Pavlov considered Communism a “doomed”
experiment that had turned Russia back into

00:19:57.190 --> 00:20:02.080
a nation of serfs, and he saw Bolshevism as
a system of government that devalued culture

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:04.590
and the intellectual strength of a nation.

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:08.440
How did Lenin treat this rogue, but celebrated
scientist?

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:12.950
At first, Pavlov and his family were treated
like any other Soviet citizens, which means

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:17.580
many of their assets were confiscated as property
of the State, including the Nobel Prize money

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:20.840
… money that could have come in handy for
the tough times that lay ahead.

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:26.010
From 1917 to 1920, the revolutionary government
had to defend itself against the White Armies

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:30.899
in the Russian Civil War, a situation that
caused generalised famine and fuel shortages

00:20:30.899 --> 00:20:32.370
for Russian citizens.

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:37.180
Like most residents of Petrograd, the Pavlovs
struggled to feed themselves and keep from

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:41.789
freezing, as at least a third of Pavlov’s
colleagues at the Academy of Sciences starved

00:20:41.789 --> 00:20:43.510
or froze to death.

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:47.470
Pavlov barely struggled to keep his family
alive by growing potatoes and other vegetables

00:20:47.470 --> 00:20:52.190
right outside his lab, and by collecting small
amounts of firewood from a colleague.

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:56.769
In 1920, Pavlov wrote to Lenin’s secretary,
seeking permission to emigrate to Western

00:20:56.769 --> 00:21:01.600
Europe or America, in search of better living
conditions and more funding for his lab.

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:06.269
Lenin immediately realised the negative PR
repercussions of losing the country’s most

00:21:06.269 --> 00:21:07.269
celebrated scientist.

00:21:07.269 --> 00:21:11.710
He ordered Petrograd Party leaders to increase
rations for Pavlov and his family, and to

00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:13.970
make sure his working conditions improved.

00:21:13.970 --> 00:21:18.070
Ivan Pavlov had prevailed over Lenin thanks
to his prestige.

00:21:18.070 --> 00:21:20.190
Would he have the same luck with his successor?

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:25.789
Josef Stalin started his climb to absolute
power after Lenin’s death in 1924, and by

00:21:25.789 --> 00:21:31.850
1927 he had launched one of his many purges,
focused on intellectuals and scientists.

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:36.380
Pavlov was outraged, especially when restrictive
measures were aimed at Jewish scientists.

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:40.480
Now, in his private life, Pavlov was as anti-Semitic
as Stalin.

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:43.740
In his view, Trotsky was a
“vile yid”

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:47.649
And in 1928, Pavlov had complained to an American
colleague that

00:21:47.649 --> 00:21:52.360
“Jews occupy high positions everywhere … it’s
a shame that the Russians cannot be rulers

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:53.640
of their own land.”

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:57.590
But it is undisputed that the scientist had
a strong sense of justice.

00:21:57.590 --> 00:22:02.019
At a time when vocal dissent may land you
in a Gulag, or shot in the back of the head

00:22:02.019 --> 00:22:07.340
by a secret police goon, Pavlov wrote to Stalin
complaining against the purge, saying that

00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:09.850
he was
“ashamed to be called a Russian.”

00:22:09.850 --> 00:22:14.539
We don’t know Stalin’s reaction to such
complaints, but we can speculate that he was

00:22:14.539 --> 00:22:15.600
not amused.

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:20.299
Luckily for Pavlov, he could rely on powerful
allies: Nikolai Bukharin, chairman of the

00:22:20.299 --> 00:22:24.250
Comintern’s executive committee, considered
Pavlov indispensable and defended his case

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:29.120
with Politburo members:
“I know that he does not sing the ‘Internationale,’

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:34.649
but despite all his grumbling, ideologically
(in his works, not in his speeches) he is

00:22:34.649 --> 00:22:35.720
working for us.”

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:41.289
In a case of pragmatism triumphing over paranoia,
Stalin agreed with Bucharin and ensured the

00:22:41.289 --> 00:22:43.399
Party would continue supporting Pavlov.

00:22:43.399 --> 00:22:47.960
The scientist and his labs continued to prosper,
even in the years leading up to Stalin’s

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:49.089
Great Purge.

00:22:49.089 --> 00:22:53.930
Pavlov expanded his operations to three laboratories,
staffed with hundreds of scientists and lab

00:22:53.930 --> 00:22:58.270
assistants, and was even permitted to collaborate
with scholars in Europe and America.

00:22:58.270 --> 00:23:01.130
But his relationship with the government was
never easy.

00:23:01.130 --> 00:23:05.269
Apparently Moscow officials did not understand
why their Big Boss allowed Pavlov to

00:23:05.269 --> 00:23:09.600
“Spit on the Soviets”
… as politburo member Valerian Kuibyshev

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:10.740
put it.

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:15.380
Kuibyshev, who was Chairman of the State Planning
Committee, and had influence over government

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:20.679
investment in agriculture, industry and science,
tried to oppose Pavlov as much as he could,

00:23:20.679 --> 00:23:23.670
at least within the limits of what Stalin
allowed him to do.

00:23:23.670 --> 00:23:27.930
This meant that Kuibyshev was forced anyways
to fund Pavlov’s laboratories, but had no

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:30.010
intention of ever honouring him.

00:23:30.010 --> 00:23:32.750
When a proposal was floated to celebrate Pavlov’s
80th anniversary as an affair of State, Kuibyshev

00:23:32.750 --> 00:23:33.750
put his foot down: no cake for him!!!

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:34.750
At least not sponsored by the State.

00:23:34.750 --> 00:23:36.919
After all, couldn’t they just play him a
metronome or something?

00:23:36.919 --> 00:23:41.309
That should do, right?

00:23:41.309 --> 00:23:44.419
Legacy
For a while, Kuibyshev successfully prevented

00:23:44.419 --> 00:23:50.130
excessive displays of admiration towards the
Nobel Laureate, but by 1934, the hostile official

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:52.000
had been removed from his post.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:54.750
Because that’s how Stalin rolled.

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:59.490
A good occasion to publicly celebrate the
pride of Russian science arrived on the 27th

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:04.429
of February, 1936, although I am sure Pavlov
would have preferred to avoid it.

00:24:04.429 --> 00:24:05.429
Why?

00:24:05.429 --> 00:24:07.049
Well, this occasion was his death.

00:24:07.049 --> 00:24:11.200
Ivan Pavlov worked in his laboratory until
the very end, even after contracting a double

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:13.539
pneumonia which eventually claimed his life.

00:24:13.539 --> 00:24:17.580
The Soviet Government honoured the great scientist
by organising a grand funeral, at which a

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:22.490
hundred thousand mourners, including Party
officials, filed past his casket.

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:28.220
Ivan Petrovich Pavlov was a physiologist until
his very last day on Earth, and yet the impact

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:31.429
of his work was most felt in Psychology.

00:24:31.429 --> 00:24:35.610
His methods may have not been the most ethical,
particularly when it came to our modern standards

00:24:35.610 --> 00:24:37.809
surrounding matters like animal cruelty.

00:24:37.809 --> 00:24:42.610
More broadly, the classical conditioning theories
he spawned, as well as behaviourist psychology

00:24:42.610 --> 00:24:48.389
in general, were criticised by later generations
of psychologists for being too reductive.

00:24:48.389 --> 00:24:52.600
Even Pavlov did not fully agree with strict
classical conditioning theory, as it did not

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:56.220
allow for the individual conscience to play
a role in human behaviour.

00:24:56.220 --> 00:25:01.470
Pavlov’s true enduring legacy lies in the
use of a rigorous scientific method to prove

00:25:01.470 --> 00:25:06.960
the existence of conditioned and non-conditioned
reflexes, which provided a foundation for

00:25:06.960 --> 00:25:33.350
the study of acquired human behaviour and
the use of positive reinforcement in learning.

